<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Fife</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Kingdom of Fife</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Fife is a council area and historic county of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, a peninsula between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/firth-of-tay">Firth of Tay</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/firth-of-forth">Firth of Forth</a>. It takes its name from the Pictish province of Fib and was held by the powerful earls of Fife in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>. Its towns include Dunfermline, burial place of Scottish kings, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-andrews">St Andrews</a>, the country's ecclesiastical capital and home of its oldest university.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/fife.jpg" alt="Fife" width="320" height="222" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kim Traynor · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Crail,_Fife,_Scotland.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Fife is a natural peninsula in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, bordered by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/firth-of-tay">Firth of Tay</a> to the north, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a> to the east and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/firth-of-forth">Firth of Forth</a> to the south. The Ochil Hills partly block the route to the west. The coast has small harbours, from the docks at Burntisland and Rosyth to the fishing villages of the East Neuk such as Anstruther and Pittenweem, with their stepped gables.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Pictish king list and the Poppleton manuscript mention a division of the Pictish realm into seven sub-kingdoms, one of them Fife, recorded as Fib in 1150. The hill-fort of Clatchard Craig near Newburgh was an important Pictish stronghold between the sixth and eighth centuries. The epithet Kingdom of Fife is first recorded only in 1678, and may derive from the quasi-regal privileges of the Earl of Fife. Until the 15th century the earl was considered the principal peer of the Scottish realm and had the right to crown the nation's monarchs.</p><p>Fife became an important royal centre from the reign of King Malcolm III, who had his principal home at Dunfermline. Dunfermline Abbey replaced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iona">Iona</a> as the burial place of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>'s royal elite, and Robert I is buried there. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-stuart">House of Stuart</a> built a palace at Falkland, favouring Fife for its hunting grounds. James VI called the county a beggar's mantle fringed wi gowd, the fringe being a coast of small ports trading wool, linen, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a> and salt with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/low-countries">Low Countries</a>.</p><p>Fife became a centre of heavy industry in the 19th century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">Coal</a> had been mined since at least the 12th century, and the number of pits increased ten-fold in the Victorian period. The Forth and Tay rail bridges linked it with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edinburgh">Edinburgh</a> and Dundee, and Kirkcaldy became the world centre of linoleum production. After the Second World War Glenrothes became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>'s second new town. The county was governed by a county council from 1890 until 1975, when Fife region was created, and Fife Council became a unitary authority in 1996.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Fife is a council area with 373,990 residents in 2025, administered from Glenrothes. Its largest settlement is Dunfermline, which was awarded city status in 2022, and its largest town on the north-east coast is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-andrews">St Andrews</a>. The University of St Andrews is the oldest of the ancient universities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, and the Old Course there is the world's oldest golf course. Fife has 4,961 listed buildings, among them Falkland Palace, Culross Palace, Dunfermline Abbey and St Andrews Cathedral.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Fife” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A later medieval tract names seven provinces, Cait (Caithness), Ce, Fib (<b>Fife</b>), Fidach, Fotla (Atholl), Circinn and Fortriu, and a list of Pictish kings survives in later copies.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-picts">Kingdom of the Picts · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>A 2023 study of eight whole genomes from Lundin Links in <b>Fife</b> and Balintore in Easter Ross found broad affinities with Iron Age Britons and present-day people of western Scotland, Wales, Northern Ireland and Northumbria, supporting a local origin.</blockquote><a href="/culture/picts">Picts · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Its territory corresponded broadly to modern <b>Fife</b> and Kinross, between the Firth of Tay and the Firth of Forth.</blockquote><a href="/state/fib">Fib</a></li><li><blockquote>The Venicones appear in Ptolemy's Geography of about 150, generally placed in <b>Fife</b> and perhaps Kinross and southern Angus, between the Forth and the Tay, with a settlement called Orrea.</blockquote><a href="/state/venicones">Venicones</a></li></ul></section>
